IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `cpr-dates`

-- Court Procedures Rules 2006 (ACT) -- the questions of the person bringing a claim (the plaintiff).
--
-- SEAT: the plaintiff: the person who brings a civil claim in the ACT Supreme Court or Magistrates Court -- for damages
-- for a personal injury, for a relative's death, for a debt -- and meets the rules on starting the proceeding, keeping
-- it alive, the defendant's time to respond, default judgment, offers of compromise, interest and costs. "On what day did
-- my proceeding start?", "Is my claim still valid for service?", "Has my proceeding been dismissed?", "By when must the
-- defendant file a defence?", "Can I get default judgment?", "Can I still accept that offer?", "What costs follow the
-- offer I made?", "Can I get interest after the defendant's offer?", "Can I start the death claim myself?". The court
-- and the registrar get no seat: what they decide is asked as a fact ("if the court ...").
--
-- Every question takes plain facts (yes/no, a number, a date, a choice from a list). No question takes a DEONTIC or an
-- EVENT. The two duties with deadlines the plaintiff waits on (the defendant's defence, r 102; the offeror's payment
-- after acceptance, r 1002 (6)) are regulative rules run inside questions.
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: r 32 (when a proceeding starts), r 74 (6) and r 76 (5) (start day for any time limit); r 74
-- (duration and renewal of originating process); r 75 and r 76 (3) (proceeding taken to be dismissed, reinstated by
-- filing); r 55 (abandoning the excess to sue in the Magistrates Court); r 102 (1), r 111 (3)-(5) (time for the
-- defence); r 1117 (defendant in default), r 1118 (1), r 1120-r 1126 (which default judgment); r 1121 and sch 3 pt 3.2
-- (default judgment costs without assessment); r 1101-r 1102 and sch 3 pt 3.1 (stay of a debt claim on payment); Part
-- 2.10 (offers of compromise): r 1002 (5), (6), (9), r 1003, r 1004 (1) (a), r 1009-r 1014; r 1619 (7)-(8) (interest
-- after a settlement offer); r 1620 (1)-(2) (interest after judgment); sch 2 (interest rates up to and after judgment);
-- r 1725 (costs where a Supreme Court judgment was within the Magistrates Court's jurisdiction); r 250 (death claims
-- for the family); r 6350 (3) (the Christmas days) in the day counts. Everything else is out: NOTES.md.
--
-- Edition: Court Procedures Rules 2006 (ACT), SL2006-29, Republication No 73 (R73), effective 1 August 2026, deposited
-- at ../../registers/source-bundle/SL2006-29.txt. The interest rates and costs amounts are in its own schedules 2 and 3.
--
-- (c) Australian Capital Territory. Used under CC BY 4.0 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md). Unofficial text.
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. Judgments the rules hand to the court are INPUTS. Answers follow the
-- rulings on negatives of 2026-11-04 and 2026-11-05 (NOTES.md): an answer that is the text's own no says so; an answer
-- where the text is limited by its own wording, or silent, is marked "NOTHING-kind" (or is NOTHING).

§ `Court Procedures Rules 2006 (ACT): the questions of the person bringing a claim`

DECLARE `Court` IS ONE OF
    `the Supreme Court`
    `the Magistrates Court`

§§ `Starting the proceeding (r 32, r 74 (6), r 76 (5))`

-- r 32 (1): a proceeding "starts on the day the originating process for the proceeding is filed"; (2) an oral
-- originating application under r 37 starts it "on the day the oral originating application is made"; (3) a later
-- lapse, renewal, dismissal or reinstatement does not matter. r 74 (6) and r 76 (5) say the same "for any time limit
-- (including a limitation period)". A filed document first rejected is taken filed on the day first filed (r 6145 (5),
-- note to r 32): the day asked is that first day.
@export On what day did my civil proceeding in an ACT court start, including for a limitation period (Court Procedures Rules 2006 (ACT) r 32, r 74 (6), r 76 (5))? It starts on the day the originating claim or application was filed, even if it later lapsed, was renewed, dismissed or reinstated.
GIVEN `oral` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you started the proceeding by an oral originating application to the Supreme Court under rule 37 (urgent cases), rather than by filing an originating claim or originating application
      `filed on` IS A DATE @desc The day your originating claim or originating application was first filed in the court (if the registry rejected it and you filed again, the day you first filed it)
      `made on` IS A DATE @desc If you made an oral originating application, the day you made it (otherwise any date; it is not used)
GIVETH A DATE
`r 32 -- on what day did my proceeding start` `oral` `filed on` `made on` MEANS
    IF `oral` THEN `made on` ELSE `filed on`

§§ `Keeping the originating process alive (r 74)`

-- r 74 (1): "valid for service for 1 year starting on the day it is filed". The last day is the day before the same
-- date a year on, worked as the anniversary of the day before filing (filed 1 March 2026: last day 28 February 2027).
-- r 74 (3): the court may renew it "for a further period, of not longer than 6 months at a time, starting on the day
-- after the day the process would otherwise end". Whether to renew is the court's (reasonable efforts to serve, or
-- another good reason): asked as a fact, with the last day of the renewal the court gave.
GIVEN `filed on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`r 74 (1) -- the last day of the first year of validity` `filed on` MEANS
    (`filed on` `plus days` (0 MINUS 1)) `plus months` 12

DECLARE `Can I still serve it` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the originating process is still valid for service (r 74 (1))`
    `yes: the originating process is valid for service under the renewal the court gave (r 74 (3))`
    `no: the originating process is no longer valid for service (r 74 (1)); you may apply to renew it even now (r 74 (4)), or start a fresh proceeding (r 74 (7))`

@export Is my originating claim (or originating application) still valid for service on the defendant (Court Procedures Rules 2006 (ACT) r 74)? It is valid for 1 year starting on the day it was filed, and for any renewal the court has given (up to 6 months at a time).
GIVEN `filed on` IS A DATE @desc The day the originating process was filed in the court
      `renewed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has renewed the originating process under rule 74
      `renewed to` IS A DATE @desc If it was renewed, the last day of the latest renewal the court gave (otherwise any date; it is not used)
      `serve on` IS A DATE @desc The day you would serve it (for example today)
GIVETH A `Can I still serve it`
`r 74 -- can I still serve my originating process` `filed on` `renewed` `renewed to` `serve on` MEANS
    IF `serve on` `is on or before` (`r 74 (1) -- the last day of the first year of validity` `filed on`)
    THEN `yes: the originating process is still valid for service (r 74 (1))`
    ELSE IF `renewed` AND `serve on` `is on or before` `renewed to`
         THEN `yes: the originating process is valid for service under the renewal the court gave (r 74 (3))`
         ELSE `no: the originating process is no longer valid for service (r 74 (1)); you may apply to renew it even now (r 74 (4)), or start a fresh proceeding (r 74 (7))`

-- r 74 (3): a renewal starts the day after the day the process would otherwise end and is "not longer than 6 months at
-- a time". The latest last day the court can give is 6 calendar months on from the day it would otherwise end (fork F3).
@export If I ask the court to renew my originating process, what is the latest day a single renewal can run to (Court Procedures Rules 2006 (ACT) r 74 (3))? A renewal starts the day after the process would otherwise end and runs for not longer than 6 months; whether to renew is the court's decision.
GIVEN `ends on` IS A DATE @desc The last day on which the originating process is (or was) valid for service: 1 year from filing less a day, or the last day of the latest renewal
GIVETH A DATE
`r 74 (3) -- the latest day one renewal can run to` `ends on` MEANS
    `ends on` `plus months` 6

§§ `Proceeding taken to be dismissed (r 75, r 76 (3))`

-- r 75 (1) (a): dismissed against a defendant if, "at the end of 1 year after the day the originating process is
-- issued", no affidavit of service on that defendant has been filed; (1) (b): if, at the end of 1 year after service,
-- the defendant has filed no notice of intention to respond or defence, no judgment has been entered and the proceeding
-- has not otherwise been disposed of; (2): in relation to a party who takes no step before the end of 1 year after the
-- last step in the proceeding. (4): dismissed "on the day after the day the relevant 1-year period ... ends" (the
-- rule's example: last step 1 July 2006, dismissed 2 July 2007). "Issued" is read as the day of filing and sealing
-- (fork F6). r 76 (3): a dismissal under r 75 (2) is undone if a party files a document in the proceeding before the end
-- of 1 year after the dismissal. r 75 (5): the plaintiff must pay the defendant's costs of a dismissed proceeding,
-- unless it is reinstated (r 75 (6)).
GIVEN `the day` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the end of 1 year after` `the day` MEANS `the day` `plus months` 12

DECLARE `Is my proceeding taken to be dismissed` IS ONE OF
    `taken to be dismissed against the defendant: no affidavit of service was filed within 1 year after the originating process was issued (r 75 (1) (a)); you must pay the defendant's costs (r 75 (5)) unless the court reinstates it (r 76)`
    `taken to be dismissed against the defendant: the defendant filed nothing, and no judgment was entered, within 1 year after service (r 75 (1) (b)); you must pay the defendant's costs (r 75 (5)) unless the court reinstates it (r 76)`
    `taken to be dismissed: no step was taken within 1 year after the last step (r 75 (2)); a party filing a document within 1 year after the dismissal reinstates it (r 76 (3)), or the court may (r 76 (2))`
    `dismissed under r 75 (2), but reinstated by a document filed within 1 year after the dismissal (r 76 (3)); for any time limit it is taken never to have been dismissed (r 76 (5))`
    `not taken to be dismissed on these facts (r 75: none of its conditions is met at this day)`

@export Is my ACT civil proceeding taken to be dismissed for delay, and if so on what ground (Court Procedures Rules 2006 (ACT) r 75, r 76 (3))? It is dismissed against a defendant if no affidavit of service is filed within a year after the originating process was issued, or if a year after service the defendant has filed nothing and there is no judgment; and in relation to a party who takes no step for a year after the last step.
GIVEN `issued on` IS A DATE @desc The day the originating process was filed and sealed (issued)
      `affidavit of service filed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an affidavit of service of the originating process on this defendant has been filed in the court
      `affidavit filed on` IS A DATE @desc If so, the day it was filed (otherwise any date; it is not used)
      `served` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the originating process has been served on this defendant
      `served on` IS A DATE @desc If so, the day it was served (otherwise any date; it is not used)
      `defendant responded` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant has filed a notice of intention to respond or a defence
      `responded on` IS A DATE @desc If so, the day the defendant first filed one (otherwise any date; it is not used)
      `judgment or disposed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether judgment has been entered against this defendant, or the proceeding has otherwise been disposed of against them
      `disposed on` IS A DATE @desc If so, the day (otherwise any date; it is not used)
      `last step on` IS A DATE @desc The day the last step in the proceeding was taken by any party (filing a notice of intention to proceed counts as a step, r 75 (3))
      `document filed after dismissal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether any party has filed a document in the proceeding after the day the proceeding would be dismissed for want of a step
      `document filed on` IS A DATE @desc If so, the day it was filed (otherwise any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc The day you are asking about (for example today)
GIVETH A `Is my proceeding taken to be dismissed`
`r 75 -- is my proceeding taken to be dismissed` `issued on` `affidavit of service filed` `affidavit filed on` `served` `served on` `defendant responded` `responded on` `judgment or disposed` `disposed on` `last step on` `document filed after dismissal` `document filed on` `today` MEANS
    IF `the 1 (a) period has ended`
       AND NOT (`affidavit of service filed` AND `affidavit filed on` `is on or before` `the 1 (a) end`)
    THEN `taken to be dismissed against the defendant: no affidavit of service was filed within 1 year after the originating process was issued (r 75 (1) (a)); you must pay the defendant's costs (r 75 (5)) unless the court reinstates it (r 76)`
    ELSE IF `served` AND `the 1 (b) period has ended`
            AND NOT (`defendant responded` AND `responded on` `is on or before` `the 1 (b) end`)
            AND NOT (`judgment or disposed` AND `disposed on` `is on or before` `the 1 (b) end`)
         THEN `taken to be dismissed against the defendant: the defendant filed nothing, and no judgment was entered, within 1 year after service (r 75 (1) (b)); you must pay the defendant's costs (r 75 (5)) unless the court reinstates it (r 76)`
         ELSE IF (`the end of 1 year after` `last step on`) `falls before` `today`
              THEN IF `document filed after dismissal`
                      AND `document filed on` `is on or before` `today`
                      AND `document filed on` `is on or before` (`the end of 1 year after` `the 2 dismissal day`)
                   THEN `dismissed under r 75 (2), but reinstated by a document filed within 1 year after the dismissal (r 76 (3)); for any time limit it is taken never to have been dismissed (r 76 (5))`
                   ELSE `taken to be dismissed: no step was taken within 1 year after the last step (r 75 (2)); a party filing a document within 1 year after the dismissal reinstates it (r 76 (3)), or the court may (r 76 (2))`
              ELSE `not taken to be dismissed on these facts (r 75: none of its conditions is met at this day)`
    WHERE
        `the 1 (a) end` MEANS `the end of 1 year after` `issued on`
        `the 1 (a) period has ended` MEANS `the 1 (a) end` `falls before` `today`
        `the 1 (b) end` MEANS `the end of 1 year after` `served on`
        `the 1 (b) period has ended` MEANS `the 1 (b) end` `falls before` `today`
        `the 2 dismissal day` MEANS (`the end of 1 year after` `last step on`) `plus days` 1

-- r 75 (4): the day a proceeding is taken to be dismissed for want of a step: the day after the 1-year period ends.
@export If no step is taken in my ACT civil proceeding, on what day is it taken to be dismissed (Court Procedures Rules 2006 (ACT) r 75 (2), (4))? The next step must be taken within 1 year after the last step; the proceeding is dismissed on the day after that year ends.
GIVEN `last step on` IS A DATE @desc The day the last step in the proceeding was taken by any party
GIVETH A DATE
`r 75 (4) -- the day my proceeding would be dismissed for want of a step` `last step on` MEANS
    (`the end of 1 year after` `last step on`) `plus days` 1

§§ `Suing in the Magistrates Court for more than its limit (r 55)`

-- r 55 (1): "applies if a person has a cause of action against another person for more than $250 000"; (2) the person
-- may start in the Magistrates Court if the person abandons the excess, limits the claim to $250 000 and says so; (3)
-- judgment is in full discharge of all demands on the cause of action. A cause of action of $250 000 or less is outside
-- r 55 (its jurisdiction is in the Magistrates Court Act 1930, not deposited): NOTHING-kind.
DECLARE `Can I sue in the Magistrates Court by abandoning the excess` IS ONE OF
    `yes, for at most $250 000, if you abandon the excess and say so in the claim; the judgment discharges the whole cause of action (r 55)`
    `not under r 55: it lets you sue in the Magistrates Court only if you abandon the excess and state that in the claim (the text's no)`
    `r 55 does not apply: the cause of action is not for more than $250 000; whether the Magistrates Court can hear it is a question for the Magistrates Court Act 1930, not these rules (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Can I bring my claim for more than $250,000 in the ACT Magistrates Court by giving up the excess (Court Procedures Rules 2006 (ACT) r 55)? You may, if you abandon everything above $250,000 and say so in the claim; the judgment then settles the whole cause of action.
GIVEN `the amount` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount of your cause of action against the defendant, in Australian dollars
      `abandon the excess` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you will abandon the excess over $250,000, limit the claim to $250,000 and state in the claim that the excess is abandoned
GIVETH A `Can I sue in the Magistrates Court by abandoning the excess`
`r 55 -- can I sue in the Magistrates Court by abandoning the excess` `the amount` `abandon the excess` MEANS
    IF `the amount` AT MOST 250000
    THEN `r 55 does not apply: the cause of action is not for more than $250 000; whether the Magistrates Court can hear it is a question for the Magistrates Court Act 1930, not these rules (NOTHING-kind)`
    ELSE IF `abandon the excess`
         THEN `yes, for at most $250 000, if you abandon the excess and say so in the claim; the judgment discharges the whole cause of action (r 55)`
         ELSE `not under r 55: it lets you sue in the Magistrates Court only if you abandon the excess and state that in the claim (the text's no)`

§§ `The defendant's time to respond (r 102, r 111)`

-- r 102 (1): the defendant must file any notice of intention to respond and defence "not later than the later of" (a)
-- 28 days after the day the claim is served and (b) if the defendant's application under r 40 to set the claim aside is
-- refused, 7 days after the refusal. r 111 (4), (5): a conditional notice of intention to respond becomes unconditional
-- if no r 40 application is made within 28 days after it is filed (or on refusal of one), and the defence is then due
-- "not later than 14 days after the day the conditional notice becomes an unconditional notice". r 6350 (3) leaves the
-- days from 25 December to 1 January out of each count (fork F4). A conditional notice that lapses for want of an
-- application becomes unconditional at the end of the 28 days: read as the day after the 28th day (fork F7).
DECLARE `The defendant's conditional notice` IS ONE OF
    `no conditional notice of intention to respond was filed`
    `a conditional notice was filed and no application under rule 40 followed within 28 days`
    `a conditional notice was filed and the defendant's application under rule 40 was refused`

GIVEN `a` IS A DATE, `b` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the later of` `a` `and` `b` MEANS IF `a` `falls before` `b` THEN `b` ELSE `a`

@export By what day must the defendant file a defence to my originating claim in an ACT court (Court Procedures Rules 2006 (ACT) r 102 (1), r 111)? Usually 28 days after service; 7 days after the court refuses the defendant's application to set the claim aside, if that is later; or 14 days after a conditional notice of intention to respond becomes unconditional. Days from 25 December to 1 January are not counted.
GIVEN `served on` IS A DATE @desc The day the originating claim was served on the defendant
      `conditional` IS A `The defendant's conditional notice` @desc Whether the defendant filed a conditional notice of intention to respond (to challenge jurisdiction or assert an irregularity), and what happened to it
      `conditional filed on` IS A DATE @desc If the defendant filed a conditional notice, the day it was filed (otherwise any date; it is not used)
      `set-aside refused` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant applied under rule 40 to have the claim set aside and the court refused the application
      `refused on` IS A DATE @desc If so, the day the application was refused (otherwise any date; it is not used)
GIVETH A DATE
`r 102 -- by what day must the defendant file a defence` `served on` `conditional` `conditional filed on` `set-aside refused` `refused on` MEANS
    CONSIDER `conditional`
    WHEN `a conditional notice was filed and no application under rule 40 followed within 28 days`
        THEN ((`conditional filed on` `plus counted days` 28) `plus days` 1) `plus counted days` 14
    WHEN `a conditional notice was filed and the defendant's application under rule 40 was refused`
        THEN `refused on` `plus counted days` 14
    OTHERWISE `the r 102 day`
    WHERE
        `the 28-day limit` MEANS `served on` `plus counted days` 28
        `the 7-day limit` MEANS `refused on` `plus counted days` 7
        `the r 102 day` MEANS
            IF `set-aside refused` THEN `the later of` `the 28-day limit` `and` `the 7-day limit` ELSE `the 28-day limit`

-- The defendant's duty with a deadline, as a regulative rule: file a defence within the days allowed, counted on the
-- day-number timeline from the day of service. Breach is what makes the defendant "in default" (r 1117 (1) (a)).
DECLARE `Defendant` IS ONE OF `the defendant`
DECLARE `Defence step` IS ONE OF `file a defence`

GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Defendant` `Defence step`
`file a defence within the time allowed` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the defendant`
    MUST `file a defence`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the defendant` BECAUSE "no defence filed within the time r 102 or r 111 allows (or the further period agreed or allowed): the defendant is in default (r 1117)"

DECLARE `Defence timing` IS ONE OF
    `the defence was filed in time`
    `the time for the defence has not yet ended`
    `the time for the defence has ended without a defence`

-- Runs the rule. Day 0 is the day of service; the defence (if filed) is an event on its day; today is a WAITUNTIL.
GIVEN `served on` IS A DATE, `last day` IS A DATE, `filed` IS A BOOLEAN, `filed on` IS A DATE, `today` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `Defence timing`
`the defence timing` `served on` `last day` `filed` `filed on` `today` MEANS
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`file a defence within the time allowed` (`the days from` `served on` `to` `last day`)) 0 `the events`
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN `the defence was filed in time`
    OTHERWISE IF `last day` `falls before` `today`
              THEN `the time for the defence has ended without a defence`
              ELSE `the time for the defence has not yet ended`
    WHERE
        `the filing day` MEANS IF `the days from` `served on` `to` `filed on` LESS THAN 0 THEN 0 ELSE `the days from` `served on` `to` `filed on`
        `the today day` MEANS `the days from` `served on` `to` `today`
        `the events` MEANS
            IF `filed` AND `filed on` `is on or before` `today`
            THEN LIST (EVENT `the defendant` `file a defence` `the filing day`), (WAITUNTIL `the today day`)
            ELSE LIST (WAITUNTIL `the today day`)

§§ `Default by the defendant (r 1117, r 1118, r 1120-r 1126)`

-- r 1117 (1) (a): in default if (i) no notice of intention to respond or defence is filed within the r 102 time "or any
-- further period agreed ... or allowed by the court"; (ii) a notice is filed but no defence within that time; (iii) a
-- conditional notice became unconditional and no defence within the r 111 time; (iv) the defence is struck out. (2) (a):
-- not in default if the proceeding is stayed on payment (r 1102), the defendant files an acknowledgment (r 1104), or the
-- defence is filed late "but before a default judgment is entered". (i) and (ii) come to the same thing: a notice alone
-- is not a defence. A defendant who submits to judgment by a r 106 notice is not covered (fork F8). r 1116: the division
-- applies only to a proceeding started by originating claim (with counterclaims and third-party notices in it).
DECLARE `Is the defendant in default` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the defendant is in default (r 1117 (1)); you may apply for default judgment and carry on against any other party (r 1118 (1))`
    `yes: the defence was struck out, so the defendant is in default (r 1117 (1) (a) (iv)); you may apply for default judgment (r 1118 (1))`
    `not yet: the time for the defence has not ended (r 1117 (1) not met)`
    `no: the defence was filed in time (r 1117 (1) not met)`
    `no: the defence was filed late but before default judgment, so the defendant is not in default (r 1117 (2) (a) (iii))`
    `no: the proceeding is stayed because the defendant paid what was claimed (r 1117 (2) (a) (i), r 1102)`
    `no: the defendant has filed an acknowledgment of the debt or liquidated demand (r 1117 (2) (a) (ii), r 1104)`
    `division 2.11.3 does not apply: the proceeding was not started by originating claim (r 1116) (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Is the defendant to my ACT claim in default, so that I can apply for default judgment (Court Procedures Rules 2006 (ACT) r 1117, r 1118)? A defendant is in default if no defence is filed within the time allowed (28 days after service, or the later time the rules or an agreement or order allow), or the defence is struck out; but not if a defence is filed late before default judgment, the claim is paid and stayed, or the debt is acknowledged.
GIVEN `by originating claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your proceeding was started by originating claim (not by originating application)
      `served on` IS A DATE @desc The day the originating claim was served on the defendant
      `conditional` IS A `The defendant's conditional notice` @desc Whether the defendant filed a conditional notice of intention to respond, and what happened to it
      `conditional filed on` IS A DATE @desc If the defendant filed a conditional notice, the day it was filed (otherwise any date; it is not used)
      `set-aside refused` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant applied under rule 40 to have the claim set aside and the court refused the application
      `refused on` IS A DATE @desc If so, the day the application was refused (otherwise any date; it is not used)
      `time extended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you agreed, or the court allowed, a further period for the defence
      `extended to` IS A DATE @desc If so, the last day of that further period (otherwise any date; it is not used)
      `defence filed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant has filed a defence
      `defence filed on` IS A DATE @desc If so, the day it was filed (otherwise any date; it is not used)
      `struck out` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has ordered the defendant's defence to be struck out
      `stayed on payment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proceeding is stayed under rule 1102 because the defendant paid the amount claimed, interest and the prescribed costs in time
      `acknowledged` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant has filed a statement acknowledging the debt or liquidated demand under rule 1104
      `today` IS A DATE @desc The day you would apply (for example today)
GIVETH A `Is the defendant in default`
`r 1117 -- is the defendant in default` `by originating claim` `served on` `conditional` `conditional filed on` `set-aside refused` `refused on` `time extended` `extended to` `defence filed` `defence filed on` `struck out` `stayed on payment` `acknowledged` `today` MEANS
    IF NOT `by originating claim`
    THEN `division 2.11.3 does not apply: the proceeding was not started by originating claim (r 1116) (NOTHING-kind)`
    ELSE IF `stayed on payment`
         THEN `no: the proceeding is stayed because the defendant paid what was claimed (r 1117 (2) (a) (i), r 1102)`
         ELSE IF `acknowledged`
              THEN `no: the defendant has filed an acknowledgment of the debt or liquidated demand (r 1117 (2) (a) (ii), r 1104)`
              ELSE IF `defence filed` AND `struck out`
                   THEN `yes: the defence was struck out, so the defendant is in default (r 1117 (1) (a) (iv)); you may apply for default judgment (r 1118 (1))`
                   ELSE CONSIDER `the defence timing` `served on` `the last day` `defence filed` `defence filed on` `today`
                        WHEN `the defence was filed in time` THEN `no: the defence was filed in time (r 1117 (1) not met)`
                        WHEN `the time for the defence has not yet ended` THEN `not yet: the time for the defence has not ended (r 1117 (1) not met)`
                        WHEN `the time for the defence has ended without a defence` THEN
                            IF `defence filed` AND `defence filed on` `is on or before` `today`
                            THEN `no: the defence was filed late but before default judgment, so the defendant is not in default (r 1117 (2) (a) (iii))`
                            ELSE `yes: the defendant is in default (r 1117 (1)); you may apply for default judgment and carry on against any other party (r 1118 (1))`
    WHERE
        `the rules' last day` MEANS `r 102 -- by what day must the defendant file a defence` `served on` `conditional` `conditional filed on` `set-aside refused` `refused on`
        `the last day` MEANS
            IF `time extended` AND `the rules' last day` `falls before` `extended to`
            THEN `extended to`
            ELSE `the rules' last day`

-- r 1120 (debt or liquidated demand, "with or without interest"), r 1122 (unliquidated damages, "with or without
-- another claim"), r 1123 (detention of goods "only"), r 1124 (possession of land "only"), r 1125 (two or more of those
-- kinds "and no other claim"), r 1126 (the plaintiff "is not entitled to apply" under r 1120, r 1122, r 1123 or r 1124).
-- A claim for unliquidated damages with another kind of claim falls under r 1122 alone; r 1126 is then shut, so the
-- other claim has no default judgment rule (finding 4, fork F9).
DECLARE `Which default judgment` IS ONE OF
    `r 1120: judgment for not more than the amount claimed, with the interest and costs stated in the affidavit in support (or costs to be agreed or assessed)`
    `r 1122: judgment for damages to be assessed (with or without your other claims); r 1126 is then not open for the other claims`
    `r 1123: judgment for the return of the goods or their value, or their value, with costs`
    `r 1124: judgment for possession of the land, with the costs of the claim and of getting judgment`
    `r 1125: judgment on all or any of your claims, each as if it were your only claim under r 1120, r 1123 or r 1124`
    `r 1126: the judgment the court considers justified on your claim, after a hearing on application with the relevant affidavits`
    `no claim for relief was stated`

GIVEN `b` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
`one if` `b` MEANS IF `b` THEN 1 ELSE 0

@export If the defendant is in default, what kind of default judgment can I apply for on my ACT claim (Court Procedures Rules 2006 (ACT) r 1120 to r 1126)? It depends on what you claim: a debt, unliquidated damages (such as personal injury damages), goods, land, a mix of these, or something else.
GIVEN `debt` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you claim a debt or liquidated demand (a fixed sum, or one worked out by calculation, with or without interest), or unliquidated damages for which rule 418 (2) lets you state an amount
      `damages` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you claim unliquidated damages (an amount for the court to assess, such as personal injury damages)
      `goods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you claim for the detention of goods
      `land` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you claim recovery of possession of land
      `other` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you claim any other relief (for example an injunction or a declaration)
GIVETH A `Which default judgment`
`r 1120 to r 1126 -- which default judgment can I ask for` `debt` `damages` `goods` `land` `other` MEANS
    IF `damages`
    THEN `r 1122: judgment for damages to be assessed (with or without your other claims); r 1126 is then not open for the other claims`
    ELSE IF `other`
         THEN `r 1126: the judgment the court considers justified on your claim, after a hearing on application with the relevant affidavits`
         ELSE IF `the kinds` GREATER THAN 1
              THEN `r 1125: judgment on all or any of your claims, each as if it were your only claim under r 1120, r 1123 or r 1124`
              ELSE IF `debt`
                   THEN `r 1120: judgment for not more than the amount claimed, with the interest and costs stated in the affidavit in support (or costs to be agreed or assessed)`
                   ELSE IF `goods`
                        THEN `r 1123: judgment for the return of the goods or their value, or their value, with costs`
                        ELSE IF `land`
                             THEN `r 1124: judgment for possession of the land, with the costs of the claim and of getting judgment`
                             ELSE `no claim for relief was stated`
    WHERE
        `the kinds` MEANS `one if` `debt` PLUS `one if` `goods` PLUS `one if` `land`

§§ `Fixed costs on a debt claim (r 1102, r 1121, schedule 3)`

-- Schedule 3, table 3.1 (prescribed costs amount, claim for debt or liquidated demand) and table 3.2 (default
-- judgment), by court and amount claimed. The Magistrates Court bands stop at "< $250 000": a claim of exactly $250 000
-- (which r 55 (2) (a) itself produces) falls in no band, and the answer is NOTHING (finding 1).
GIVEN `the court` IS A `Court`, `the amount claimed` IS A NUMBER, `the column` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`the schedule 3 amount` `the court` `the amount claimed` `the column` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the court` EQUALS `the Supreme Court` THEN JUST (IF `the column` EQUALS 1 THEN 1538 ELSE 1975)
           IF `the amount claimed` LESS THAN 10000 THEN JUST (IF `the column` EQUALS 1 THEN 507 ELSE 658)
           IF `the amount claimed` LESS THAN 25000 THEN JUST (IF `the column` EQUALS 1 THEN 1031 ELSE 1316)
           IF `the amount claimed` LESS THAN 40000 THEN JUST (IF `the column` EQUALS 1 THEN 1232 ELSE 1580)
           IF `the amount claimed` LESS THAN 50000 THEN JUST (IF `the column` EQUALS 1 THEN 1384 ELSE 1779)
           IF `the amount claimed` LESS THAN 250000 THEN JUST (IF `the column` EQUALS 1 THEN 1538 ELSE 1975)
           OTHERWISE NOTHING

-- r 1102 (2), r 1103 (3), r 1121 via r 1725: a Supreme Court proceeding that "could properly have been brought in the
-- Magistrates Court" takes the Magistrates Court amount. Whether it could is asked as a fact.
GIVEN `the court` IS A `Court`, `could have been in the Magistrates Court` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Court`
`the court whose amount applies` `the court` `could have been in the Magistrates Court` MEANS
    IF `could have been in the Magistrates Court` THEN `the Magistrates Court` ELSE `the court`

-- r 1101: division 2.11.1 "applies to a proceeding that (a) is for a debt or liquidated demand only; and (b) is started by
-- originating claim" -- a scope limit with no "only" on the division itself: outside it, NOTHING. r 1102 (1): the
-- proceeding is stayed if, within the time for filing a notice of intention to respond or defence, the defendant pays the
-- amount claimed, any interest claimed, and the prescribed costs amount plus filing and service fees actually paid.
@export How much must the defendant pay me, within the time for filing a defence, to stop my ACT claim for a debt (Court Procedures Rules 2006 (ACT) r 1102 and schedule 3, part 3.1)? The amount claimed, the interest claimed, the fixed costs amount for the court and claim size, and the filing and service fees you actually paid. Gives nothing where the rule does not reach the claim.
GIVEN `debt only` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your proceeding is for a debt or liquidated demand only (a fixed or calculable sum, with any interest)
      `by originating claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was started by originating claim
      `the court` IS A `Court` @desc The court your proceeding is in
      `could have been in the Magistrates Court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you are in the Supreme Court, whether the proceeding could properly have been brought in the Magistrates Court (otherwise answer no)
      `the amount claimed` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount you claim, in Australian dollars, without interest
      `the interest claimed` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount of interest you claim, in Australian dollars (0 if none)
      `the fees paid` IS A NUMBER @desc The filing and service fees you actually paid, in Australian dollars
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`r 1102 -- what must the defendant pay to stay my debt claim` `debt only` `by originating claim` `the court` `could have been in the Magistrates Court` `the amount claimed` `the interest claimed` `the fees paid` MEANS
    IF NOT (`debt only` AND `by originating claim`)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE CONSIDER `the schedule 3 amount` (`the court whose amount applies` `the court` `could have been in the Magistrates Court`) `the amount claimed` 1
         WHEN JUST `the costs amount` THEN JUST (`the amount claimed` PLUS `the interest claimed` PLUS `the costs amount` PLUS `the fees paid`)
         WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

-- r 1121 (2): on a default judgment for a debt or liquidated demand only, the costs and disbursements claimed (other than
-- filing and service fees) are allowed without assessment if "not more than" the schedule 3, part 3.2 amount; (4) they
-- must be agreed or assessed if more. r 1121 (3) makes (2) subject to r 1725 (Supreme Court judgment within the
-- Magistrates Court's jurisdiction): where r 1725 applies, the answer gives way (NOTHING; fork F10).
@export Will the costs I claim on a default judgment for a debt be allowed without assessment (Court Procedures Rules 2006 (ACT) r 1121 and schedule 3, part 3.2)? They are, if they are not more than the fixed amount for the court and claim size (filing and service fees are added on top); if more, they must be agreed or assessed.
GIVEN `the court` IS A `Court` @desc The court your proceeding is in
      `the amount claimed` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount you claim, in Australian dollars, without interest
      `the costs claimed` IS A NUMBER @desc The costs and disbursements you claim, in Australian dollars, not counting the filing and service fees you actually paid
      `rule 1725 applies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you are in the Supreme Court, whether the Magistrates Court could have heard the proceeding (apart from the amount) and judgment is for less than $175,000 (otherwise answer no)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`r 1121 -- are my default judgment costs allowed without assessment` `the court` `the amount claimed` `the costs claimed` `rule 1725 applies` MEANS
    IF `rule 1725 applies` AND `the court` EQUALS `the Supreme Court`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE CONSIDER `the schedule 3 amount` `the court` `the amount claimed` 2
         WHEN JUST `the costs amount` THEN JUST (`the costs claimed` AT MOST `the costs amount`)
         WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

§§ `Costs where a Supreme Court judgment was within the Magistrates Court's jurisdiction (r 1725)`

-- r 1725 (1): applies to a Supreme Court proceeding the Magistrates Court could have heard (or could have, apart from
-- the amount), where the plaintiff is entitled to costs and judgment is for less than $175 000 (excluding costs). (2):
-- the plaintiff gets "only" the Magistrates Court determined fee plus (b) under $50 000: 50% of the Supreme Court
-- disbursements; (c) $50 000 to under $100 000: 50% of the Supreme Court costs and disbursements; (d) $100 000 to under
-- $175 000: 75%. (3): the court may order a different amount -- asked as a fact. The Supreme Court amounts are those for
-- a judgment of more than $250 000 (the rule's own measure).
DECLARE `What costs do I get` IS ONE OF
    `r 1725 does not apply: the judgment is not one it covers; your costs follow the ordinary rules (NOTHING-kind)`
    `the Magistrates Court determined fee, and 50% of the disbursements (not the costs) you could have recovered in the Supreme Court on a judgment over $250 000 (r 1725 (2) (a), (b))`
    `the Magistrates Court determined fee, and 50% of the costs and disbursements you could have recovered in the Supreme Court on a judgment over $250 000 (r 1725 (2) (a), (c))`
    `the Magistrates Court determined fee, and 75% of the costs and disbursements you could have recovered in the Supreme Court on a judgment over $250 000 (r 1725 (2) (a), (d))`
    `the amount the court ordered instead (r 1725 (3))`

@export I won my claim in the ACT Supreme Court for less than $175,000, in a case the Magistrates Court could have heard: what costs do I get (Court Procedures Rules 2006 (ACT) r 1725)? Only the Magistrates Court filing fee and a share (50% or 75%) of the Supreme Court costs or disbursements, by the size of the judgment, unless the court orders otherwise.
GIVEN `could have been in the Magistrates Court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Magistrates Court would have had jurisdiction and power to hear and decide the proceeding (or would have, apart from the amount claimed)
      `entitled to costs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are entitled to the costs of the proceeding
      `the judgment amount` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount of the judgment for you (including a judgment by consent), in Australian dollars, excluding costs
      `the court ordered otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has ordered that you are entitled to a different amount for costs and disbursements under rule 1725 (3)
GIVETH A `What costs do I get`
`r 1725 -- what costs do I get on a Supreme Court judgment under $175,000` `could have been in the Magistrates Court` `entitled to costs` `the judgment amount` `the court ordered otherwise` MEANS
    IF NOT (`could have been in the Magistrates Court` AND `entitled to costs` AND `the judgment amount` LESS THAN 175000)
    THEN `r 1725 does not apply: the judgment is not one it covers; your costs follow the ordinary rules (NOTHING-kind)`
    ELSE IF `the court ordered otherwise`
         THEN `the amount the court ordered instead (r 1725 (3))`
         ELSE IF `the judgment amount` LESS THAN 50000
              THEN `the Magistrates Court determined fee, and 50% of the disbursements (not the costs) you could have recovered in the Supreme Court on a judgment over $250 000 (r 1725 (2) (a), (b))`
              ELSE IF `the judgment amount` LESS THAN 100000
                   THEN `the Magistrates Court determined fee, and 50% of the costs and disbursements you could have recovered in the Supreme Court on a judgment over $250 000 (r 1725 (2) (a), (c))`
                   ELSE `the Magistrates Court determined fee, and 75% of the costs and disbursements you could have recovered in the Supreme Court on a judgment over $250 000 (r 1725 (2) (a), (d))`

§§ `Offers of compromise (Part 2.10)`

-- r 1000: Part 2.10 is subject to the Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002, ch 14 (limits on legal costs) and the Road Transport
-- (Third-Party Insurance) Act 2008, pts 4.8 and 4.9. Those Acts are not brought in (fork F11): where they apply, the
-- answers below may be displaced.

-- r 1002 (5): "The end of a period of acceptance for an offer -- (a) for an offer made 2 months or more before the date
-- set down for the start of the trial -- must be not less than 28 days after the day the offer is made; and (b) in any
-- other case -- must be after a period that is reasonable in the circumstances." Read literally, an offer made before
-- any trial date is set falls under (b) (finding 2, fork F12). Whether a period is reasonable is the court's: asked.
-- r 6350 (3) is not applied: r 1002 (5) says what an offer must state, not what must be done within a period (fork F4).
@export Is the acceptance period in an offer of compromise long enough (Court Procedures Rules 2006 (ACT) r 1002 (5))? For an offer made 2 months or more before the trial date, the period must end at least 28 days after the day the offer is made; otherwise it must be reasonable in the circumstances.
GIVEN `made on` IS A DATE @desc The day the offer was made
      `trial date set` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a date has been set down for the start of the trial
      `trial starts on` IS A DATE @desc If so, the date set down for the start of the trial (otherwise any date; it is not used)
      `period ends on` IS A DATE @desc The last day of the period of acceptance stated in the offer
      `reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the offer is made less than 2 months before the trial date (or no trial date is set): whether the court would find the period reasonable in the circumstances (otherwise any answer; it is not used)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `r 1002 (5) -- is the acceptance period long enough` `made on` `trial date set` `trial starts on` `period ends on` `reasonable` IF
    IF `trial date set` AND `made on` `is on or before` (`trial starts on` `plus months` (0 MINUS 2))
    THEN (`made on` `plus days` 28) `is on or before` `period ends on`
    ELSE `reasonable`

-- r 1003 (1): an offer may be accepted "at any time during the period of acceptance"; r 1002 (9): it may not be
-- withdrawn during that period "unless the court orders otherwise". Outside the period, the text's no (periods ruling
-- (a)).
@export Can I still accept the offer of compromise I received (Court Procedures Rules 2006 (ACT) r 1003 (1), r 1002 (9))? You can accept, by serving written notice on the person who made it, at any time during its period of acceptance, even if a further offer has been made; the offer cannot be withdrawn in that period unless the court orders.
GIVEN `period ends on` IS A DATE @desc The last day of the period of acceptance stated in the offer
      `withdrawn by court order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has allowed the offer to be withdrawn and it has been withdrawn
      `accept on` IS A DATE @desc The day you would serve your notice of acceptance (for example today)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `r 1003 -- can I still accept the offer` `period ends on` `withdrawn by court order` `accept on` IF
        `accept on` `is on or before` `period ends on`
    AND NOT `withdrawn by court order`

-- r 1002 (6): unless the offer says otherwise, payment is "within 28 days after the offer is accepted"; r 1004 (1) (a):
-- the accepting party may withdraw the acceptance if the money is not paid to the offeree or into court within 28 days
-- after acceptance or the other time the offer provides; (b) or with the court's leave. r 6350 (3) applied (fork F4).
-- A payment after the deadline does not take away the right (fork F13).
DECLARE `Offeror` IS ONE OF `the party who made the offer`
DECLARE `Offer step` IS ONE OF `pay the amount of the accepted offer`

GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Offeror` `Offer step`
`pay within the time after acceptance` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the party who made the offer`
    MUST `pay the amount of the accepted offer`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the party who made the offer` BECAUSE "not paid to the offeree or into court within the time after acceptance: the offeree may withdraw the acceptance (r 1004 (1) (a))"

@export Can I withdraw my acceptance of an offer of compromise because the money was not paid (Court Procedures Rules 2006 (ACT) r 1004 (1), r 1002 (6))? Unless the offer gives another time, the money must be paid to you or into court within 28 days after you accepted (days from 25 December to 1 January not counted); if it is not, or if the court gives leave, you may withdraw your acceptance by written notice.
GIVEN `accepted on` IS A DATE @desc The day you accepted the offer
      `other time in offer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offer provides a time for payment other than 28 days after acceptance
      `days in offer` IS A NUMBER @desc If so, the number of days after acceptance the offer allows (otherwise any number; it is not used)
      `paid` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the money has been paid to you or into court
      `paid on` IS A DATE @desc If so, the day it was paid (otherwise any date; it is not used)
      `court leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has given you leave to withdraw the acceptance
      `today` IS A DATE @desc The day you would withdraw (for example today)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `r 1004 -- can I withdraw my acceptance for non-payment` `accepted on` `other time in offer` `days in offer` `paid` `paid on` `court leave` `today` IF
        `court leave`
    OR  `the payment time ended unpaid` `accepted on` `the last day` `paid` `paid on` `today`
    WHERE
        `the last day` MEANS IF `other time in offer` THEN `accepted on` `plus counted days` `days in offer` ELSE `accepted on` `plus counted days` 28

-- Runs the payment rule. Day 0 is the day of acceptance; the payment (if made) is an event on its day; today is a
-- WAITUNTIL. FULFILLED: paid in time. Otherwise the right to withdraw arises once the last day has passed.
GIVEN `accepted on` IS A DATE, `last day` IS A DATE, `paid` IS A BOOLEAN, `paid on` IS A DATE, `today` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the payment time ended unpaid` `accepted on` `last day` `paid` `paid on` `today` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`pay within the time after acceptance` (`the days from` `accepted on` `to` `last day`)) 0 `the events`
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE `last day` `falls before` `today`
    WHERE
        `the today day` MEANS `the days from` `accepted on` `to` `today`
        `the paying day` MEANS IF `the days from` `accepted on` `to` `paid on` LESS THAN 0 THEN 0 ELSE `the days from` `accepted on` `to` `paid on`
        `the events` MEANS
            IF `paid` AND `paid on` `is on or before` `today`
            THEN LIST (EVENT `the party who made the offer` `pay the amount of the accepted offer` `the paying day`), (WAITUNTIL `the today day`)
            ELSE LIST (WAITUNTIL `the today day`)

-- r 1009 to r 1014: the costs that follow an offer. The plaintiff's seat: "I" is the plaintiff.
-- r 1009: accepted and no provision for costs. r 1010: the plaintiff's offer not accepted and the plaintiff gets a
-- judgment "no less favourable" than the offer. r 1011: the defendant's offer not accepted and the plaintiff gets a
-- judgment "no more favourable". r 1012: the defendant's offer not accepted and the defendant obtains a judgment "no less
-- favourable to the defendant" -- read as a judgment the defendant obtains (the claim dismissed or judgment for the
-- defendant), so that it does not overlap r 1011 (fork F14). r 1013: interest for the period after the offer is
-- disregarded in the comparison. r 1014 (3): if the offeror was not shown to be willing and able to carry out the offer,
-- r 1010 and r 1011 do not apply. "Unless the court orders otherwise" throughout: if it does, NOTHING-kind. Whether the
-- claim is a "personal injury claim" (undefined in the rules) is asked. A judgment for the defendant on the defendant's
-- unaccepted offer is r 1012 whatever the offer's amount (fork F14).
DECLARE `Who made the offer` IS ONE OF `I made the offer`, `the defendant made the offer`

DECLARE `How the claim ended` IS ONE OF
    `the offer was accepted`
    `judgment for me`
    `judgment for the defendant, or my claim dismissed`

DECLARE `The costs that follow the offer` IS ONE OF
    `r 1009 (2): you get your costs from the defendant, party and party, up to the time the offer was made`
    `r 1009 (3): the defendant gets its costs from you, party and party, up to the time the offer was made`
    `r 1010 (2) (a): you get your costs from the defendant on a solicitor and client basis for the whole proceeding (personal injury claim)`
    `r 1010 (2) (b): you get your costs from the defendant, party and party until the switch day, and solicitor and client from it`
    `r 1011 (2) (a): you get your costs, party and party, up to and including the day of the offer, and none after; you pay none of the defendant's costs from that day (personal injury claim)`
    `r 1011 (2) (b): you get your costs, party and party, until the switch day; from it the defendant gets its costs from you, party and party`
    `r 1012: the defendant gets its costs from you, party and party until the switch day, and solicitor and client from it`
    `Part 2.10 gives no costs order on these facts: the offer made provision for costs, or the judgment did not meet the offer, or the offeror was not shown willing and able (r 1014 (3)); costs follow the ordinary rules (NOTHING-kind)`
    `the court ordered otherwise: Part 2.10's costs rule gives way to its order (NOTHING-kind)`

@export What costs follow an offer of compromise in my ACT claim (Court Procedures Rules 2006 (ACT) r 1009 to r 1014)? It depends on who made the offer, whether it was accepted, how the judgment compares with it (ignoring interest for the time after the offer), and whether it is a personal injury claim.
GIVEN `who` IS A `Who made the offer` @desc Who made the offer of compromise: you (the plaintiff) or the defendant
      `outcome` IS A `How the claim ended` @desc Whether the offer was accepted; or, if not, whether judgment was given for you or for the defendant (including your claim being dismissed)
      `offer provides for costs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the offer was accepted: whether it made provision for costs (otherwise any answer; it is not used)
      `the offer amount` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount of the judgment the offer proposed for you, in Australian dollars (0 if it proposed judgment for the defendant)
      `the judgment amount` IS A NUMBER @desc If judgment was for you: its amount in Australian dollars, including any interest (otherwise 0)
      `interest after the offer` IS A NUMBER @desc The part of the judgment that is interest, or damages in the nature of interest, for the period after the day the offer was made (0 if none)
      `personal injury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is a personal injury claim
      `willing and able` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the other side asked under rule 1014 that the offeror show it was at all times willing and able to carry out the offer: whether the court was satisfied it was (answer yes if no one asked)
      `the court ordered otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has ordered otherwise about these costs
GIVETH A `The costs that follow the offer`
`Part 2.10 -- what costs follow the offer` `who` `outcome` `offer provides for costs` `the offer amount` `the judgment amount` `interest after the offer` `personal injury` `willing and able` `the court ordered otherwise` MEANS
    IF `the court ordered otherwise`
    THEN `the court ordered otherwise: Part 2.10's costs rule gives way to its order (NOTHING-kind)`
    ELSE CONSIDER `outcome`
         WHEN `the offer was accepted` THEN
             IF `offer provides for costs`
             THEN `no costs order from Part 2.10`
             ELSE IF `the offer amount` GREATER THAN 0
                  THEN `r 1009 (2): you get your costs from the defendant, party and party, up to the time the offer was made`
                  ELSE `r 1009 (3): the defendant gets its costs from you, party and party, up to the time the offer was made`
         WHEN `judgment for me` THEN
             IF NOT `willing and able`
             THEN `no costs order from Part 2.10`
             ELSE IF `who` EQUALS `I made the offer`
                  THEN IF `the compared amount` AT LEAST `the offer amount`
                       THEN IF `personal injury`
                            THEN `r 1010 (2) (a): you get your costs from the defendant on a solicitor and client basis for the whole proceeding (personal injury claim)`
                            ELSE `r 1010 (2) (b): you get your costs from the defendant, party and party until the switch day, and solicitor and client from it`
                       ELSE `no costs order from Part 2.10`
                  ELSE IF `the compared amount` AT MOST `the offer amount`
                       THEN IF `personal injury`
                            THEN `r 1011 (2) (a): you get your costs, party and party, up to and including the day of the offer, and none after; you pay none of the defendant's costs from that day (personal injury claim)`
                            ELSE `r 1011 (2) (b): you get your costs, party and party, until the switch day; from it the defendant gets its costs from you, party and party`
                       ELSE `no costs order from Part 2.10`
         WHEN `judgment for the defendant, or my claim dismissed` THEN
             IF `who` EQUALS `the defendant made the offer`
             THEN `r 1012: the defendant gets its costs from you, party and party until the switch day, and solicitor and client from it`
             ELSE `no costs order from Part 2.10`
    WHERE
        `the compared amount` MEANS `the judgment amount` MINUS `interest after the offer`
        `no costs order from Part 2.10` MEANS `Part 2.10 gives no costs order on these facts: the offer made provision for costs, or the judgment did not meet the offer, or the offeror was not shown willing and able (r 1014 (3)); costs follow the ordinary rules (NOTHING-kind)`

-- The "switch day" of r 1010 (2) (b) (ii), r 1011 (2) (b) (ii) and r 1012 (2) (b). r 1010 and r 1011: if the offer was
-- made before the first day of the trial, "from the day the period for acceptance of the offer ends"; r 1012: "on and
-- from the day after the offer was made" (finding 3: r 1012 switches earlier). Made on or after the first day of trial:
-- "at and from 11 am on the day after the offer was made", in all three.
DECLARE `The rule that sets the switch` IS ONE OF
    `r 1010 or r 1011 (judgment for me)`
    `r 1012 (judgment for the defendant)`

DECLARE `The switch` IS ONE OF
    `from the start of` HAS `switch day` IS A DATE
    `at and from 11 am on` HAS `switch day at 11 am` IS A DATE

@export From when do the higher or shifted costs run after an offer of compromise (Court Procedures Rules 2006 (ACT) r 1010 (2) (b), r 1011 (2) (b), r 1012 (2))? For an offer made before the first day of the trial: from the day its acceptance period ends (r 1010, r 1011), or from the day after the offer (r 1012); for an offer made on or after the first day of the trial: from 11 am on the day after the offer.
GIVEN `the rule` IS A `The rule that sets the switch` @desc Which costs rule applies: r 1010 or r 1011 (you got judgment), or r 1012 (the defendant got judgment on its offer)
      `made on` IS A DATE @desc The day the offer was made
      `trial began on` IS A DATE @desc The first day of the trial
      `period ends on` IS A DATE @desc The last day of the offer's period of acceptance
GIVETH A `The switch`
`r 1010 to r 1012 -- from when do the offer costs change` `the rule` `made on` `trial began on` `period ends on` MEANS
    IF `made on` `falls before` `trial began on`
    THEN IF `the rule` EQUALS `r 1012 (judgment for the defendant)`
         THEN `from the start of` (`made on` `plus days` 1)
         ELSE `from the start of` `period ends on`
    ELSE `at and from 11 am on` (`made on` `plus days` 1)

§§ `Interest (r 1619, r 1620, schedule 2)`

-- r 1619 (7): in a proceeding for damages, no interest "in relation to a period after the defendant offers (or first
-- offers) an appropriate settlement amount ... unless the special circumstances of the case justify" it. (8): "if" the
-- judgment (including interest until the day of the offer) "does not exceed the amount offered ... by more than 10%, the
-- amount offered is an appropriate settlement amount". (8) is a sufficient test, not a definition: where the judgment
-- exceeds the offer by more than 10%, the rules do not say whether the offer was otherwise appropriate (NOTHING-kind).
DECLARE `Interest after the defendant's offer` IS ONE OF
    `no: the offer was an appropriate settlement amount (r 1619 (8)), so the court must not order interest for the period after it (r 1619 (7))`
    `yes, the court may: the offer was an appropriate settlement amount, but the special circumstances of the case justify interest after it (r 1619 (7))`
    `r 1619 (8) does not settle it: the judgment exceeds the offer by more than 10%; whether the offer was still an appropriate settlement amount is not said (NOTHING-kind)`
    `r 1619 (7) does not bar it: the defendant made no settlement offer`
    `r 1619 (7) does not apply: not a proceeding for damages (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Can the court give me interest for the time after the defendant offered to settle my ACT damages claim (Court Procedures Rules 2006 (ACT) r 1619 (7), (8))? Not after an appropriate settlement offer, unless special circumstances justify it; an offer is appropriate if the judgment (with interest to the day of the offer) is not more than 10% above it.
GIVEN `for damages` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your proceeding is a proceeding for damages
      `offered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant offered an amount in settlement of the proceeding
      `the offer` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount the defendant first offered in settlement, in Australian dollars (0 if none)
      `the judgment to the offer day` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount of your judgment, including interest up to the day of the offer (but not after it), in Australian dollars
      `special circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds that the special circumstances of the case justify ordering interest after the offer
GIVETH A `Interest after the defendant's offer`
`r 1619 (7) -- can I get interest after the defendant's offer` `for damages` `offered` `the offer` `the judgment to the offer day` `special circumstances` MEANS
    IF NOT `for damages`
    THEN `r 1619 (7) does not apply: not a proceeding for damages (NOTHING-kind)`
    ELSE IF NOT `offered`
         THEN `r 1619 (7) does not bar it: the defendant made no settlement offer`
         ELSE IF (`the judgment to the offer day` MINUS `the offer`) TIMES 10 AT MOST `the offer`
              THEN IF `special circumstances`
                   THEN `yes, the court may: the offer was an appropriate settlement amount, but the special circumstances of the case justify interest after it (r 1619 (7))`
                   ELSE `no: the offer was an appropriate settlement amount (r 1619 (8)), so the court must not order interest for the period after it (r 1619 (7))`
              ELSE `r 1619 (8) does not settle it: the judgment exceeds the offer by more than 10%; whether the offer was still an appropriate settlement amount is not said (NOTHING-kind)`

-- r 1620 (1): interest is payable on an unpaid judgment debt (other than costs), unless the court otherwise orders; (2)
-- not "if the amount is paid in full not later than 28 days after the day the judgment takes effect". r 6350 (3) applied
-- (fork F4). The usual order as to interest (r 1622, with its Medicare and Centrelink conditions) is out (NOTES.md).
DECLARE `Interest on my judgment` IS ONE OF
    `none: the judgment was paid in full within 28 days after it took effect (r 1620 (2))`
    `not yet decided: the 28 days have not ended and the judgment is not paid in full (r 1620 (2))`
    `yes: interest is payable on the unpaid amount at the schedule 2, part 2.2 rate (or the rate in your claim, after a r 1120 default judgment) (r 1620 (1))`
    `the court ordered otherwise: r 1620 gives way to its order (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Will interest run on my ACT court judgment for money (Court Procedures Rules 2006 (ACT) r 1620 (1), (2))? Interest is payable on an unpaid judgment debt, but none if the judgment is paid in full within 28 days after it takes effect (days from 25 December to 1 January not counted), unless the court orders otherwise.
GIVEN `takes effect on` IS A DATE @desc The day the judgment takes effect
      `paid in full` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the judgment debt (not counting costs) has been paid in full
      `paid on` IS A DATE @desc If so, the day it was paid in full (otherwise any date; it is not used)
      `the court ordered otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has made a different order about interest on the judgment
      `today` IS A DATE @desc The day you are asking about (for example today)
GIVETH A `Interest on my judgment`
`r 1620 -- will interest run on my judgment` `takes effect on` `paid in full` `paid on` `the court ordered otherwise` `today` MEANS
    IF `the court ordered otherwise`
    THEN `the court ordered otherwise: r 1620 gives way to its order (NOTHING-kind)`
    ELSE IF `paid in full` AND `paid on` `is on or before` `the last day` AND `paid on` `is on or before` `today`
         THEN `none: the judgment was paid in full within 28 days after it took effect (r 1620 (2))`
         ELSE IF `today` `is on or before` `the last day`
              THEN `not yet decided: the 28 days have not ended and the judgment is not paid in full (r 1620 (2))`
              ELSE `yes: interest is payable on the unpaid amount at the schedule 2, part 2.2 rate (or the rate in your claim, after a r 1120 default judgment) (r 1620 (1))`
    WHERE
        `the last day` MEANS `takes effect on` `plus counted days` 28

-- Schedule 2, part 2.1 (interest up to judgment) and part 2.2 (after judgment): tables 2.1 to 2.4 for periods to 30 June
-- 2010, and from 1 July 2010 the cash rate last published by the Reserve Bank of Australia before the start of the
-- half-year (1 January or 1 July) plus 4 points (up to judgment) or 6 points (after judgment). The cash rate is not in
-- the rules: it is asked. Rates are percentages per year. Before table 2.1's first period (1 January 1974) the
-- Supreme Court table gives no rate: NOTHING. r 1619 (5) lets the court set the rate "having regard to" this one.
DECLARE `Interest kind` IS ONE OF
    `interest up to judgment`
    `interest after judgment`

@export What rate of interest do the ACT court rules set for a given day, up to judgment or after judgment (Court Procedures Rules 2006 (ACT) schedule 2)? For days after 30 June 2010 it is the Reserve Bank cash rate last published before the half-year began, plus 4 points (up to judgment) or plus 6 points (after judgment); earlier days use the schedule's tables.
GIVEN `the kind` IS AN `Interest kind` @desc Whether you want the rate of interest up to judgment (schedule 2, part 2.1) or after judgment (part 2.2)
      `the court` IS A `Court` @desc The court the proceeding is in
      `the day` IS A DATE @desc The day for which you want the rate
      `the cash rate` IS A NUMBER @desc For a day after 30 June 2010: the Reserve Bank of Australia cash rate, in percent, last published before the start of the half-year (1 January or 1 July) that contains the day (otherwise any number; it is not used)
      `judgment entered on` IS A DATE @desc For interest after judgment on a day before 1 May 1986: the day judgment was entered (otherwise any date; it is not used)
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`schedule 2 -- what is the interest rate` `the kind` `the court` `the day` `the cash rate` `judgment entered on` MEANS
    IF NOT (`the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 1 7 2010))
    THEN JUST (IF `the kind` EQUALS `interest up to judgment` THEN `the cash rate` PLUS 4 ELSE `the cash rate` PLUS 6)
    ELSE IF `the kind` EQUALS `interest up to judgment`
         THEN IF `the court` EQUALS `the Supreme Court` THEN `table 2.1` ELSE JUST `table 2.2`
         ELSE IF `the court` EQUALS `the Supreme Court` THEN JUST `table 2.3` ELSE JUST `table 2.4`
    WHERE
        `table 2.1` MEANS
            BRANCH IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 1 1 1974) THEN NOTHING
                   IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 1 1 1981) THEN JUST 10
                   IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 1 1 1986) THEN JUST 14
                   IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 1 1 1988) THEN JUST 18
                   IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 1 1 1990) THEN JUST 16
                   IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 1 7 1990) THEN JUST 21
                   IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 1 1 1991) THEN JUST 18
                   IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 1 7 1991) THEN JUST 16
                   IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 1 7 1993) THEN JUST 15
                   IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 1 5 2001) THEN JUST 10
                   OTHERWISE JUST 9
        `table 2.2` MEANS
            BRANCH IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 1 7 1981) THEN 13.375
                   IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 1 7 1982) THEN 15
                   IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 1 1 1983) THEN 17.5
                   IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 1 1 1984) THEN 15.5
                   IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 1 7 1984) THEN 12.375
                   IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 1 1 1985) THEN 14.75
                   IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 1 7 1985) THEN 13.75
                   IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 1 1 1986) THEN 17.25
                   IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 1 7 1986) THEN 20.625
                   IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 1 1 1987) THEN 18.125
                   IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 1 7 1987) THEN 19.25
                   IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 1 3 1988) THEN 18.75
                   IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 1 3 1989) THEN 16.125
                   IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 1 9 1989) THEN 17.875
                   IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 15 1 1991) THEN 20.75
                   IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 15 7 1991) THEN 17.5
                   IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 15 1 1992) THEN 15.75
                   IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 15 7 1992) THEN 13.75
                   IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 15 1 1993) THEN 11.5
                   IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 1 2 1994) THEN 10
                   IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 1 8 1995) THEN 9
                   IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 1 4 1997) THEN 10.75
                   IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 1 5 1998) THEN 9.55
                   IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 10 1 2005) THEN 8.45
                   OTHERWISE 9
        `the old after-judgment rate` MEANS
            IF `judgment entered on` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 1 10 1977) THEN 5 ELSE 10
        `table 2.3` MEANS
            BRANCH IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 1 5 1986) THEN `the old after-judgment rate`
                   IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 1 7 1990) THEN 15
                   IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 1 1 1992) THEN 20
                   IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 1 7 1993) THEN 15
                   IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 1 5 2001) THEN 12
                   OTHERWISE 11
        `table 2.4` MEANS
            BRANCH IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 1 5 1986) THEN `the old after-judgment rate`
                   IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 1 7 1990) THEN 15
                   IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 1 1 1992) THEN 20
                   IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 1 7 1993) THEN 15
                   IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 10 4 1995) THEN 12
                   IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 1 8 1995) THEN 9
                   IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 1 4 1997) THEN 10.75
                   IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 1 5 1998) THEN 9.55
                   IF `the day` `falls before` (`the calendar date` 10 1 2005) THEN 8.45
                   OTHERWISE 11

§§ `Death claims for the family (r 250)`

-- r 250 (1): "Not more than 1 proceeding" under the Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002, pt 3.1 may be started against a person
-- in relation to a death (a flat negative: the text's no). (2): it "must be started by the deceased person's personal
-- representative" for the family members who suffered damage or loss (the beneficiaries). (3): "if the proceeding has
-- not been started by the personal representative at the end of 6 months after the day of the death, any 1 or more of
-- the beneficiaries may start the proceeding" -- read as from the day after the 6-month period ends (fork F15). Who is a
-- member of the family is the Civil Law (Wrongs) Act's: asked.
DECLARE `Who I am` IS ONE OF
    `the deceased person's personal representative`
    `a member of the deceased person's family who suffered damage or loss because of the death`
    `someone else`

DECLARE `Can I start the death claim` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the personal representative starts the proceeding, for the family members who suffered loss (r 250 (2))`
    `yes: the personal representative did not start it within 6 months after the death, so you may, for the same people (r 250 (3), (4))`
    `not yet: within 6 months after the death only the personal representative may start it (r 250 (2), (3))`
    `no: a proceeding has already been started against this person for this death; only one may be (r 250 (1)); ask the court to add a name left out (r 251)`
    `no: only the personal representative or, after 6 months, a family member who suffered loss may start it (r 250 (2), (3))`

@export Can I start the court proceeding for compensation for a relative's death in the ACT (Court Procedures Rules 2006 (ACT) r 250)? Only one proceeding may be brought against a person for a death; the personal representative brings it for the family, and if they have not done so by the end of 6 months after the death, any family member who suffered loss may.
GIVEN `who` IS A `Who I am` @desc Who you are in relation to the person who died
      `died on` IS A DATE @desc The day of the death
      `already started` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a proceeding for this death under the Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002, part 3.1 has already been started against the person you would sue (by anyone)
      `start on` IS A DATE @desc The day you would start the proceeding (for example today)
GIVETH A `Can I start the death claim`
`r 250 -- can I start the death claim` `who` `died on` `already started` `start on` MEANS
    IF `already started`
    THEN `no: a proceeding has already been started against this person for this death; only one may be (r 250 (1)); ask the court to add a name left out (r 251)`
    ELSE CONSIDER `who`
         WHEN `the deceased person's personal representative` THEN `yes: the personal representative starts the proceeding, for the family members who suffered loss (r 250 (2))`
         WHEN `a member of the deceased person's family who suffered damage or loss because of the death` THEN
             IF (`died on` `plus months` 6) `falls before` `start on`
             THEN `yes: the personal representative did not start it within 6 months after the death, so you may, for the same people (r 250 (3), (4))`
             ELSE `not yet: within 6 months after the death only the personal representative may start it (r 250 (2), (3))`
         WHEN `someone else` THEN `no: only the personal representative or, after 6 months, a family member who suffered loss may start it (r 250 (2), (3))`
